			<h1>What is udm=14?</h1>
			<p class="intro">The URL parameter that gives you classic Google search results without AI.</p>

			<nav class="toc">
				<h2>Contents</h2>
				<ol>
					<li><a href="#what">What udm=14 Does</a></li>
					<li><a href="#history">How It Was Discovered</a></li>
					<li><a href="#other-params">Other UDM Parameters</a></li>
					<li><a href="#how-to-use">How to Use It</a></li>
					<li><a href="#automatic">Make It Automatic</a></li>
				</ol>
			</nav>

			<section id="what">
				<h2>What udm=14 Does</h2>
				<p><code>udm=14</code> is a URL parameter that forces Google to display the "Web" tab results - the classic "10 blue links" style search results that Google used before AI Overviews.</p>

				<div class="comparison">
					<div class="comparison-item">
						<h3>Without udm=14</h3>
						<ul>
							<li>AI Overview at the top</li>
							<li>Featured snippets</li>
							<li>Knowledge panels</li>
							<li>Video carousels</li>
							<li>People Also Ask boxes</li>
							<li>Mixed content types</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
					<div class="comparison-item highlight">
						<h3>With udm=14</h3>
						<ul>
							<li>Clean list of web pages</li>
							<li>No AI summaries</li>
							<li>No knowledge panels</li>
							<li>No video carousels</li>
							<li>Faster loading</li>
							<li>Just links to websites</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>

				<p>The parameter name <code>udm</code> likely stands for "User Display Mode" or similar internal Google terminology. The value <code>14</code> corresponds to the "Web" filter.</p>
			</section>

			<section id="history">
				<h2>How It Was Discovered</h2>
				<p>When Google added the "Web" filter tab to search results (as an alternative to "All", "Images", "Videos", etc.), users noticed that clicking it changed the URL. The parameter <code>udm=14</code> appeared.</p>

				<p>Tech-savvy users realized they could append this parameter directly to any Google search URL to bypass AI Overview and go straight to web-only results.</p>

				<p>The discovery spread through:</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Hacker News discussions</li>
					<li>Reddit threads (r/google, r/technology)</li>
					<li>Tech blogs and Twitter/X</li>
					<li>Tools like <a href="https://udm14.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">udm14.com</a> and <a href="https://tenbluelinks.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tenbluelinks.org</a></li>
				</ul>

				<p>The parameter is not officially documented by Google but is stable because it's used by Google's own UI for the Web tab filter.</p>
			</section>

			<section id="other-params">
				<h2>Other UDM Parameters</h2>
				<p>Google uses various <code>udm</code> values for different content filters:</p>

				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>Parameter</th>
							<th>Filter</th>
							<th>Description</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>udm=2</code></td>
							<td>Images</td>
							<td>Shows image results</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>udm=6</code></td>
							<td>Videos</td>
							<td>Shows video results</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>udm=7</code></td>
							<td>News</td>
							<td>Shows news articles</td>
						</tr>
						<tr class="highlight-row">
							<td><code>udm=14</code></td>
							<td>Web</td>
							<td>Classic links, no AI</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>udm=28</code></td>
							<td>Forums</td>
							<td>Shows forum discussions</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>

				<p>The <code>udm=14</code> (Web) filter is the only one that actively removes AI Overview, making it the go-to for users who want traditional results.</p>
			</section>

			<section id="how-to-use">
				<h2>How to Use It</h2>

				<h3>Manual Method</h3>
				<p>Add <code>&udm=14</code> to the end of any Google search URL:</p>

				<div class="code-example">
					<p>Before:</p>
					<code class="code-block">https://www.google.com/search?q=best+laptops+2024</code>
					<p>After:</p>
					<code class="code-block">https://www.google.com/search?q=best+laptops+2024<strong>&udm=14</strong></code>
				</div>

				<h3>Bookmark Method</h3>
				<p>Create a bookmark with this URL and use it as a search shortcut:</p>
				<code class="code-block">https://www.google.com/search?q=%s&udm=14</code>
				<p>In most browsers, <code>%s</code> gets replaced with your search term when you use the bookmark as a keyword search.</p>

				<h3>Custom Search Engine</h3>
				<p>Add a custom search engine in your browser settings:</p>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>Name:</strong> Google (No AI)</li>
					<li><strong>Keyword:</strong> g or gw</li>
					<li><strong>URL:</strong> <code>https://www.google.com/search?q=%s&udm=14</code></li>
				</ul>
				<p>Then type <code>g your search</code> in the address bar to search without AI.</p>
			</section>

			<section id="automatic">
				<h2>Make It Automatic</h2>
				<p>Don't want to think about parameters? These options apply <code>udm=14</code> automatically:</p>

				<div class="option-card recommended">
					<span class="option-tag">Easiest</span>
					<h3>Use Unslop Search</h3>
					<p>Search on <a href="/">unslopsearch.com</a> and we add the parameter for you. Set it as your default search engine for automatic AI-free results.</p>
					<a href="/" class="cta-button">Try Unslop Search</a>
				</div>

				<div class="option-card">
					<h3>Browser Extension</h3>
					<p>Install our <a href="/guides/browser-extension">Chrome extension</a> to automatically add <code>udm=14</code> to every Google search you make.</p>
				</div>

				<div class="option-card">
					<h3>Third-Party Tools</h3>
					<ul>
						<li><a href="https://udm14.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">udm14.com</a> - Simple Google frontend with udm=14</li>
						<li><a href="https://tenbluelinks.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tenbluelinks.org</a> - Similar tool, helps set browser defaults</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="technical-note">
				<h2>Technical Notes</h2>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>Stability:</strong> Since <code>udm=14</code> is used by Google's own "Web" tab, it's unlikely to be removed. However, Google could change the parameter name in the future.</li>
					<li><strong>Combination:</strong> You can combine <code>udm=14</code> with other Google parameters like <code>&lr=lang_en</code> (language filter) or <code>&num=20</code> (results per page).</li>
					<li><strong>Mobile:</strong> The parameter works on mobile Google searches too.</li>
					<li><strong>Localization:</strong> Works on all Google country domains (google.co.uk, google.de, etc.)</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<div class="next-steps">
				<h2>Next Steps</h2>
